L000386	\subsection{From $t_{2g}$- to $J_{\mathrm{eff}}$- Hamiltonian}
L000387	
L000388	The $t_{2g}$-Hamiltonian of Eq.\ref{eq:se_hamiltonian} is defined in the spin+orbital many-electron basis states $|\tilde{\Gamma} (i) \rangle$, which, for the two-electron case, belong to the Hund's rule GS multiplet with the degeneracy $(2l+1)(2S+1)=9$ for $S=1$ and $l=1$.
L000389	The corresponding intersite exchange interactions ($t_{2g}$-IEI) can be downfolded onto the \jeff many-electron basis $|\Gamma (i) \rangle$. Here $i$ is the magnetic site index (1 for $d^1$ and 2 for $d^2$ for simplicity). To this end, we introduce the projector operator $P_{LS} (i) = |\Gamma (i)  \rangle \langle \tilde{\Gamma} (i)|$, such that any many-electron $t_{2g}$-shell operator $\tau_{s, l}^{\alpha, \beta}$  can be $J_{\mathrm{eff}}$-projected as
L000390	\beq
L000391	   \tilde{O}^{\alpha, \beta}_{s,l} (i)  = P_{LS} (i)\  \tau_{s, l}^{\alpha, \beta} (i) \ P^\dagger_{LS}(i).
L000392	\eeq
L000393	The multipole many-electron operators $O_K^Q$ are expanded in terms of the projected operators through coefficients   $c_{s\alpha, l\beta}^{KQ} (i) = Tr[\tilde{O}^{\alpha, \beta}_{s,l}(i) O_K^Q]$. Specifically, for the $(K,Q)=(3,-2)$ component, corresponding to the $xyz$ octupole in Cartesian coordinates, this expansion yields the values listed in Tab.~\ref{tab:expansion} for both $d^1$ and $d^2$ configurations.
L000394	
L000395	The $J_{\mathrm{eff}}$-IEI are then obtained by contracting these coefficients with the original interaction matrix elements:
L000396	\beq
L000397	V^{QQ'}_{KK'} (ij) = \sum_{s,l}\sum_{\alpha, \beta}  I^{\alpha \alpha', \beta \beta'}_{s s', l l'} (ij) c_{s\alpha, l\beta}^{KQ} (i) c_{s\alpha, l\beta}^{K'Q'} (j).
L000398	\eeq
L000399	The difference between the resulting SO-projected $t_{2g}$-IEI matrix and the initial $J_{\mathrm{eff}}$-IEI is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:endmatter1}. The SO-projected IEI reproduce the overall structure (See SM~\cite{supplmat}), with excellent agreement in the dipole–dipole channels, while higher-order multipolar terms are systematically underestimated. For instance, the octupole–octupole coupling $V^{xyz}$ is $\sim -0.6$~meV for the $d^2$–$d^2$ bond, compared to the expected $-1.6$~meV. This discrepancy likely originates from neglecting SO coupling in the excited $d^1$ and $d^3$ configurations in the superexchange processes, though further investigation is required.
